80
00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:42.000
But that's the real legacy in the blood of the families that were shed and could never, never be recovered. And Paul, that line he left on Sunday morning to see the football match. It was two o'clock. The horror of it is massively amplified by the fact that it is a leisure activity, that it should properly be a kind of safe space away from the horrors of the war. And that's why the story has such resonance and it transforms an understanding as well of the sheer brutality of this conflict.

81
00:41:42.000 --> 00:42:07.000
And what it does, I think, in general, is that it shows you the descent into brutality that was well underway by November 1920. So within a week, you have the Kilmichael ambush near McCroom where Tom Barry and his West Cork. We mentioned him, didn't we? Yeah, so Tom Barry and the former British. Yeah, he'd fought in Mesopotamia. I gather, kind of partly being radicalised by how useless he thought the British High Command were there.

82
00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:43.000
He was involved in the Kilmichael ambush in which 17 auxiliaries... Which is the worst military defeat the British suffer in the war, I think. Yes, and then a month later there's another auxiliary killed at Dillon's Cross in Cork. In response to that, signed off on, British forces go in and they burned down the city hall and they burned down the Carnegie library. So they're the kind of the high point reprisals. They're the ones that really stand out. But all across the country were small scale reprisals like attacks on private property on livestock on creamers. Creamers matter so much across rural Ireland foundation of the economy there.

83
00:42:43.000 --> 00:43:13.000
